  "text": "Solution 3 is wrong. Barwick's theorem of the heart makes $K_j(\\mathcal{C}^\\heartsuit)\\to K_j(\\mathcal{C})$ an isomorphism for all $j\\ge 0$, so the answer should be a lower bound on $j$, not an upper bound like $n-1$.\n\nSolution 1 rests on the claim that a bounded $t$-structure alone forces $K_{-1}(\\mathcal{C})=0$, which gives $j=-n$ at $n=1$. I don't think that claim holds. The Antieau–Gepner–Heller work, as I recall it, gives bounded $t$-structures with $K_{-1}\\neq 0$, and if the bound were unconditional the Ext hypothesis in the problem would be pointless. I can't check the references here, so this rests on my recollection.\n\nSolution 2 is internally consistent. Its Ext-to-K dictionary puts the first possible defect at $\\operatorname{Ext}^{n+1}$, which contributes to $K_{-n}$. So $K_{-i}(\\mathcal{C})=0$ for $i\\le n-1$, and the map is an isomorphism for $j\\ge 1-n$. At $n=1$ this gives $j\\ge 0$, which matches Barwick's theorem with no extra hypothesis. I'm not fully certain of the exact indexing, but it is the most coherent of the three.\n\nCHOICE: 2",
